2026-10-20T07:40:52.1152060Z 
2026-10-20T07:40:52.1152410Z To use the bundled libc++ please use the following LDFLAGS:
2026-10-20T07:40:52.1153020Z   LDFLAGS="-L/opt/homebrew/opt/llvm/lib/c++ -L/opt/homebrew/opt/llvm/lib/unwind -lunwind"
2026-10-20T07:40:52.1153830Z Features newer than system libc++ will require the following define to enable
2026-10-20T07:40:52.1154490Z (support for this may be removed in a future major LLVM release):
2026-10-20T07:40:52.1154950Z   CPPFLAGS="-D_LIBCPP_DISABLE_AVAILABILITY"
2026-10-20T07:40:52.1155200Z 
2026-10-20T07:40:52.1155710Z NOTE: You probably want to use the libunwind and libc++ provided by macOS unless you know what you're doing.
2026-10-20T07:40:52.1156130Z 
2026-10-20T07:40:52.1156510Z llvm is keg-only, which means it was not symlinked into /opt/homebrew,
2026-10-20T07:40:52.1157250Z because macOS already provides this software and installing another version in
2026-10-20T07:40:52.1168770Z parallel can cause all kinds of trouble.
2026-10-20T07:40:52.1169030Z 
2026-10-20T07:40:52.1169290Z If you need to have llvm first in your PATH, run:
